# MASTER BUILD PROMPT: COMPREHENSIVE MEAL MANAGEMENT PLATFORM

## 1. YOUR ROLE AND MISSION

Act as a senior full-stack software architect, lead React/TypeScript engineer, database architect, M&E specialist, evaluation researcher, statistician, data-quality expert, UX designer, cybersecurity engineer, and QA engineer.

Your mission is to transform my existing Monitoring and Evaluation (M&E) Indicator Tracker into a comprehensive, production-ready Monitoring, Evaluation, Accountability and Learning (MEAL) management platform.

This is an existing application, not a new project. You must inspect the existing codebase, preserve working functionality, improve its architecture where justified, complete unfinished modules, and integrate all the tools into a coherent system.

Do not simply add navigation items, dashboards, static cards, mock tables, placeholder pages, decorative buttons, or simulated functionality. Every feature presented as operational must have a functioning end-to-end workflow, appropriate backend persistence, validation, error handling, and access control.

The goal is to build a platform that can support real development programmes, NGOs, government institutions, international organisations, research projects, humanitarian programmes, implementing partners, M&E consultants, and social-impact organisations.

The application must support the complete programme lifecycle:

PLAN → DESIGN → COLLECT → VALIDATE → MONITOR → ANALYSE → EVALUATE → REPORT → LEARN → IMPROVE.

The platform should be modular, extensible, secure, responsive, accessible, maintainable, and capable of growing into a multi-organisation software product.

## 2. EXISTING TECHNOLOGY AND NON-NEGOTIABLE CONSTRAINTS

The existing application uses:

- React 19.
- TypeScript.
- Tailwind CSS.
- Lucide-style icons.
- Firebase Firestore for persistent storage.
- Google Gemini through the Google GenAI SDK for AI-assisted insights.
- A desktop ribbon-style navigation system organised into application, project, M&E framework, data lifecycle, insights, learning, workflow, and administration areas.

Begin by auditing the actual repository. Confirm the framework, routing, installed packages, Firestore configuration, data model, environment variables, existing components, and current functionality rather than assuming the documentation perfectly reflects the code.

### Preservation requirements

1. Preserve the existing application, its visual identity, working features, and navigation wherever practical.
2. Do not replace the existing frontend unnecessarily.
3. Do not delete or overwrite existing project records without an explicit migration and recovery plan.
4. Do not remove Firebase merely because another database is available.
5. Assess whether Firestore can adequately support the proposed data model and analytical workloads.
6. Recommend PostgreSQL/Supabase or a dedicated backend only where the benefits justify the migration or additional complexity.
7. If a database migration is recommended, document the reasons, target schema, migration procedure, validation process, rollback strategy, costs and security implications before executing it.
8. Keep existing APIs and data structures backward compatible where feasible.
9. Do not hard-code credentials, secrets, administrator passwords, API keys or privileged operations in client-side code.
10. Never claim a feature works unless it has been implemented and tested.

The final product must remain a web application usable on desktop computers, laptops, tablets and smartphones.

